    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pughtown likely takes its name from a man of Welsh descent whose land holdings helped the community grow.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pughtown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Pughtown functioned as a self-contained village with blacksmith shops, wheelwrights, a creamery, and a general store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pughtown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early settlement of Pughtown occurred after William Penn established Chester County as a haven for Quakers in 1682.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pughtown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Quaker influence in Pughtown emphasized pacifism, education, and land stewardship during the era of colonial expansion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pughtown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pughtown is located in South Coventry Township on Pennsylvania Route 100, just south of Buckstown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
